\section{Values in typed columns}{

By default every table is typed: its columns come from the record's typed
structure in the Rust \code{fec-parser} crate, so amounts are \code{double}, dates are
\code{Date}, checkboxes are \code{logical} and everything else is \code{character}. When a
field can't be read as its type:
\itemize{
\item \strong{A blank or garbage \emph{main} amount (such as \code{contribution_amount} or
\code{expenditure_amount}) is \code{0}, not \code{NA}.} This matters for \code{mean()} and
for counting rows that have no amount.
\item A blank or garbage secondary amount (such as \code{contribution_aggregate}) is
\code{NA}.
\item A blank or garbage date, including an impossible one such as \code{20230231},
is \code{NA}. Legacy \code{MM/DD/YYYY} dates are read too.
\item Blank text is \code{NA}, except for the fields the record requires (such as
\code{contributor_name_last_name} and \code{contributor_name_first_name}), which
are \code{""} when blank: \code{contributor_name_first_name} is \code{""} on every
organization's row.
}

The original text of a garbage value isn't kept. Read the filing with
\code{raw = TRUE} to see it.
