\section{Column names}{

A typed column is named by the path to its field, joined with \verb{_} and never
shortened: a contributor's last name is \code{contributor_name_last_name}, and
the address state is \code{contributor_address_state}. The names are the same
for every FEC format version, legacy ones included, so tables from
different filings stack with \code{dplyr::bind_rows()} or
\code{purrr::list_rbind()}. Every table starts with \code{filing_id}.

With \code{raw = TRUE}, the columns are the format version's own field names
(which can differ between versions). They're all \code{character}, except dates
(\code{Date}) and amounts (\code{double}).
}

\section{Rows with no typed structure}{

Nothing is dropped and nothing errors:
\itemize{
\item A record family with no typed structure (such as Schedule I), or a row
in a format version with no layout for it, becomes a table with raw
columns. If the same family also has typed rows in the filing, the raw
rows go in \verb{<name>_raw} (such as \code{schedule_a_raw}). \code{print()} marks these
tables \verb{(raw columns)}.
\item Rows that belong to no record family go in an \code{other} table, with
\code{filing_id}, \code{row_type}, \code{field_1}, \code{field_2}, ... as text.
}

A field past the end of a row's layout is kept as \code{extra_1}, \code{extra_2},
... on raw rows, and dropped on typed rows.
